# Atlas Shared Systems Lock

Treat this as architecture policy for all future shared features.

## 1) Always handle both shells

For shared features, explicitly implement both:
- website shell version
- app shell version

Do not assume one presentation works for both.

Website shell expectations:
- desktop-appropriate layout
- grid or split-panel structure where useful
- stronger horizontal-space usage
- web hierarchy, hover states, and denser information where appropriate

App shell expectations:
- mobile-first stacked flow
- sticky CTA patterns where needed
- safe-area awareness
- thumb-friendly spacing
- simplified hierarchy with fewer simultaneous decisions

Same feature, different shell behavior. Do not let one shell inherit the wrong presentation from the other.

## 2) Build shared systems once, wrap by role

For Personal features that also apply to Client:
- build one shared underlying engine/data model/rules layer
- add separate role wrappers for Personal and Client

Do not create separate core logic stacks when the human workflow is fundamentally the same.

Common shared systems include:
- nutrition targets
- macro/calorie interpretation
- workout logging
- readiness/daily check-in
- progress trends
- adjustment rules
- food logging
- adherence summaries

## 3) Role wrappers differ by permissions/copy, not core logic

Personal wrapper:
- self-directed language
- self-edit controls
- upgrade surfaces
- Enhanced/AI layers only when entitled

Client wrapper:
- coach-directed context where relevant
- coach-owned targets where appropriate
- limited self-editing when coach should control the variable
- messaging/coach feedback connections
- same quality and shared interaction logic as Personal

Client must not feel like a degraded clone.

## 4) Nutrition interpretation expansion

When building relevant nutrition feedback, interpret contextually:
- calories
- protein
- carbs
- fats

Use target + actual + user goal + training context (when available).

Examples:
- build muscle + under protein => recovery/gain concern
- lose fat + over calories => cut drift/tighten day guidance
- hard training day + under carbs => under-fueled session risk
- high fats + carbs massively over => macro distribution issue, not only calories

## 5) Competition context expansion (conditional only)

For competition-focused contexts, extend shared nutrition logic to support:
- sodium/salt
- hydration if tracked
- tighter macro adherence
- training-day fueling sensitivity

Do not force prep-level complexity onto general users. Gate this by competition/prep context.

## 6) Implementation checklist (required)

For new Personal nutrition/workout/readiness/progress features:
1. Check whether Client should inherit the same shared engine.
2. Implement explicit role-wrapper differences.
3. Implement explicit website-shell and app-shell versions.

Do not leave Client behind.
Do not let web look like stretched app.
Do not let app inherit desktop interaction patterns.

## 7) Reporting checklist (required in final response)

Always report:
- what is shared across Personal and Client
- what differs by role wrapper
- what differs between website shell and app shell
- whether competition-only logic was conditionally added
